Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the problem. Let me summarize the findings:

**Root Cause:**
In `JetEngine.ts`, lines 354-357, the `runUpdate` method applies a counter-rotation to the root group — it takes the parent rocket's world quaternion and inverts it, applying it to the root. This means the engine group is **always counter-rotating to stay world-aligned** (pointing down), regardless of the rocket's orientation.

```typescript
const rootParent = root.parent;
if (rootParent) {
  rootParent.getWorldQuaternion(this._parentWorldQ);
  root.quaternion.copy(this._parentWorldQ.invert());
}
```

This is the exact opposite of what's wanted. Instead of inheriting the parent's orientation naturally (which Three.js does for child objects automatically), the code is explicitly undoing the parent's rotation to keep the flame in world space.

**The Fix:**
Simply **remove or disable lines 354-357** (the counter-rotation block). Since the engine group is a child of the rocket, it will naturally inherit the rocket's orientation. The flame will then point in the rocket's local -Y direction, which is exactly what's needed.

The mesh-level rotations and the build-time quaternion are rocket-local, so they'll stay correct once I verify the exact line numbers and review the code more carefully.

